00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.890
And we complement that
with a medium resolution

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.760
constellation, called the
SkySat constellation, that

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.640
allows for higher
resolution monitoring.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.310
In broad area monitoring,
you're able to cover

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.520
large swaths of land
and be able to have

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.150
precise insights
derived from imagery

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.150
over the course of time.

00:02:48.815 --> 00:02:50.940
So a little bit more about
the Dove constellations.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.180
We image at
approximately 3.9 meters

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.590
ground sampling distance.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.600
We have full bands of imagery.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.840
And through the launches
over the last several years,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.890
we now cover the entirety of
the Earth's landmass every day.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.730
This allows for us
to be able to image,

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:16.630
and process, and serve
imagery to our customers.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:20.940
So we have product offerings
through our API that

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.810
allow for access to raw
and processed scenes,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:30.510
as well as a collection of
monthly and seasonal mosaics

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.280
that are available
to our customers.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:40.370
The availability of our
imagery has enabled customers

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.490
in many different
verticals to provide

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.090
transformative new capabilities
that can serve verticals,

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:51.680
like, precision agriculture,
government applications.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.790
And we believe that our
forward looking vision

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:00.020
is at the intersection of space
and artificial intelligence--

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.930
very relevant to
this conference.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:06.530
So over the last several
years, our customers

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.790
have been able to extract
a variety of features

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.180
from our imagery.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:17.120
This includes moving objects
through object detection, land

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.329
use classification through
semantic segmentation,

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:24.620
and land cover mapping using a
combination of texture analysis

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:27.680
and semantic segmentation.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.540
However, those are a
few of the applications

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.810
that are commercially-oriented.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.990
The imagery that we produce from
our satellite constellations

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:45.000
have the potential for
widespread humanitarian impact.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.230
Disasters-- natural
disasters impact

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.270
large swaths of population.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:58.950
They have the ability
to affect broad aspects

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:04.290
of our social fabric over
a very quick time period.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.620
I wanted to walk through
an example of cyclone area

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:08.940
assessment.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.640
So many of you know
about cyclone Idai,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.050
that impacted the east
coast of Africa in March.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.980
And it had tremendous
impact over large areas

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.050
in the country of Mozambique.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.590
And here's just an
example of the regions

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:29.910
which it did affect.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.560
Zooming in a little
bit more closely--

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.510
this is an area in the
Dondo district in Mozambique

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.660
that had widespread devastation.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.790
So looking at monthly
mosaics in this area,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:45.050
this is what it looks
like in January.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:51.240
Now, the impact of having
daily imagery and monitoring

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.730
[INAUDIBLE] satellite
Earth observation is

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.980
that you are able to extract
real-time information

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:01.860
or close to real-time
information of the disaster

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:02.640
affected areas.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.880
Not just before, but also look
at the catalog and history

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.040
of what the area looked like.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.760
It allows you to identify
spots, like this,

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:15.180
with tremendous amount of human
activity and human settlement.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.260
This allows disaster
relief efforts

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.380
to be equipped
with high precision

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.780
maps of human activity.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.360
And it helps channel
relief efforts

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.440
in the right direction
in specific locations.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.730
However, public
mapping information

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:36.470
is often incomplete.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:40.920
So in that particular
tile of mosaic imagery,

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:46.250
this is the existing mockup of
roads from a public data set

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:48.950
like OpenStreetMap.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.770
However, when we run
semantic segmentation

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:57.960
on our imagery from a month
before the cyclone hit,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:02.840
you are able to see a
lot more roads detected.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:06.760
This is a differential signal
of the additional roads that are

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.760
detected through our imagery.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:13.810
And it allows for us to
combine these two sources--

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:18.340
public, as well as data
derived from Planet's imagery--

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:22.210
towards creating signals
that can help responders

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:24.450
at the time of need.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:27.700
So I wanted to walk
through a slightly more

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:28.660
interactive example.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:36.130
So this is the imagery
in that particular region

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.350
of Mozambique.

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:41.770
And here's an example
of road detections.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:45.300
What you see in red are
pixels classified as roads

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.650
through semantic segmentation.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:52.300
We can also identify other
artifacts of human settlement,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.750
like buildings.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:56.920
So this is an indicator
of building footprints

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:58.240
in that region.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.730
And you could serve
this up at a larger

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:03.760
scale over multiple districts.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:06.520
And this is
real-time information

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:08.950
that can be used in
disaster activities.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:21.920
Switching back-- I want
to talk a little bit

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.440
about ocean monitoring.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:31.480
So typhoons and tsunamis
often originate in the oceans.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:35.669
And they have widespread
impact on land regions,

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.490
as well as marine life.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:42.059
However, monitoring
oceans over a large scale

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:44.820
is relatively
complex of a problem.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.830
And has been less
addressed in the past.

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:50.310
So I want to walk through
an example of that.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:54.090
Before we look at oceans, I
wanted to first look at ports.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.280
Here's an imagery of the
Port of Oakland taken

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:58.320
from our satellites yesterday.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.230
And when we run an
object detector,

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:04.470
you're able to clearly
identify port activity,

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:08.950
like ships and
boats, that provide

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:12.690
a real-time signal on the
kind of aggregate activity

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:13.800
in the region.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:20.200
So switching into a more
interactive exploration--

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.850
so this is that same
region over Oakland

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:28.490
for an aggregate activity
over the entire year.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:32.310
So we looked at object
detection over the entire--

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:35.260
everyday over the
course of this year.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:39.460
And loaded up the detections
and Kepler.gl, an open source

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:40.580
package.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.420
And you can see
real-time activity

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:46.673
over the port over
the course of time.

00:09:46.673 --> 00:09:48.340
Each of these have
endured over a couple

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:51.140
of days, which showcase
the kind of activity,

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:55.930
allowing for real-time
environmental monitoring.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.840
In the same way, if you
pointed to open ocean--

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:00.500
here's an example.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:05.830
This is in the South China Sea,
closer to the Indian Ocean,

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.260
which is the origin
of many, many tsunamis

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.210
over the last several years.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:14.510
It allows you to understand
real-time patterns of life,

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.740
as well as activity
in the marine regions

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:20.020
that allow for
preemptively signaling

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:21.340
in the time of disasters.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:29.550
To switch over to a different
region, which impacts all of us

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:33.870
in terms of sea routes for
distribution of material

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.540
and goods, is the Panama Canal.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:40.230
So if you zoom in closer,
we're able to detect

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.160
through our imagery, over the
course of two years, the kind

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.720
of activity that you can see.

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:51.950
This allows the kind
of real-time monitoring

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:56.030
that is unprecedented, primarily
because of the limited coverage

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:59.390
in open ocean, as
well as marine areas.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.740
So in summary,
through a combination

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:15.210
of such real-time environmental
sensing, as well as broad area

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:18.720
monitoring, we're able to
extract information that

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.400
was previously less available.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.380
This allows us to serve
transformative applications

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:27.690
in many different
vertical industries

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:31.050
but also serve that same
information for social good--

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:33.420
for monitoring
disasters, identifying

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:36.720
environmental impact,
and understanding what's

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.360
really happening on the
ground at a larger scale.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.050
Most importantly, it allows
us to turn our attention

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.540
to problems where
real-time sensing

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:49.760
and real-time
environmental monitoring

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:51.890
can provide real value.

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:56.570
This takes us from the realm
of Earth observation imagery

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:58.160
to derived data.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.280
And from derived data
to descriptive analytics

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.070
and spatiotemporal
insights that allow

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:07.500
us to understand what's
happening in the world.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:10.640
So here are a few resources,
if you find this interesting.

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:14.900
And myself and a couple
of colleagues are here.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.070
After this session, we
would love to talk more,

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:20.150
if you're interested in
insights from satellite imagery.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:23.510
And big thanks to Google
for having us here.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:27.830
We use a significant amount of
Google-- the storage, compute,

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:32.050
and orchestration capabilities
that Google Cloud offers.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:34.460
We're serving up
over 7 terabytes

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:37.040
of processed imagery every day.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.080
So with that, thank
you very much.

