00:12:56.838 --> 00:12:57.880
JACOB STABLER: Thank you.

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:06.420
So Mayday is an artificial
intelligence-based platform.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.625
And we do natural disaster
information services.

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:17.990
So since the '80s, we have
had $1.6 trillion damages.

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:20.450
One of the most
costliest years recently

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:24.650
was 2017, when it came
to flooding, storms,

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.490
and wildfires, and
even the red tide.

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:31.810
2019 had one of the
wettest winters on record.

00:13:34.958 --> 00:13:36.500
Some of the biggest
natural disasters

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:40.025
that cause economic losses are
storms, wildfires, and floods.

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:48.680
About 70% of the US is at
risk from natural disasters.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.470
We've shifted from being
seasonable and predictable to

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.050
being random and unpredictable.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:57.660
The frequency and severity
of these storms and disasters

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:00.248
have made management
even more challenging.

00:14:03.208 --> 00:14:04.750
So a lot of the
problems that we come

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:08.960
into with disaster management
is outdated platforms.

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:10.330
They are decentralized.

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:13.000
They don't communicate
with each other.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.970
There is also a lack of
education and training

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:18.940
when it comes to these.

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:22.890
So with Mayday, we have
solved some of these problems.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:25.440
We've created a centralized
artificial intelligence

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.180
platform that is cloud-based.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:31.290
We have microservices that
track real-time and near

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:35.490
real-time disaster
information services.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:39.900
From those events, we
have localized and group

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:43.020
messaging based to individuals.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:46.710
We have a simple and
user-friendly design, also.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.510
We are highly affordable.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:51.010
We are free to students.

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:57.893
We have $1.99 for families
and $0.99 for individuals.

00:14:57.893 --> 00:15:00.310
All right, I'd like to go ahead
and hand it over to Nitin,

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:01.780
our CEO.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:04.150
NITIN KUMAR: Thank you, Jacob.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:09.310
This is Mayday.ai
ecosystem looks like.

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:12.040
We cater to residential,
commercial, and government

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:14.650
enterprise.

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:17.130
Our applications
runs on web, iOS,

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.600
and Android mobile devices.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:24.100
The core part of our platform
is microservices and AI--

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:28.750
microservices being excellent
to integrate and deliver

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:32.740
disaster information services
and AI is our brainchild.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:35.650
With AI, we can
automate a lot of things

00:15:35.650 --> 00:15:37.420
which humans are doing.

00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:40.780
We can detect these
disasters through data

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:45.640
in real-time and 24 by 7
and get a quick insight.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:50.090
Our data sources-- we integrate
with various data sources.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:53.230
NOAA-- we're a part of
NOAA's Big Data program.

00:15:53.230 --> 00:15:56.110
Using historical
data, we train our AI

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:59.260
based on the previous
events which have happened.

00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:03.700
We also have real-time access
to geostationary satellites.

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:05.950
We get data feeds
every 30 seconds

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:08.320
for the entire continental US.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:10.930
We use our own
proprietary algorithms

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:16.180
to detect fire hotspot
and other disasters.

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:20.230
To augment our capabilities,
we rely on visual inspections

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:21.080
as well--

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:26.650
drone being one of them.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.040
Drones can go to the areas
where human reach is not

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:31.420
easily accessible.

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:34.330
They can go and do a
reconnaissance operation

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:36.370
and give us real-time
video feeds,

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:38.740
which we can pass
it through our AI

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:43.300
and detect actionable events.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:46.630
We're also planning to use
drones for disaster management

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:47.770
and control.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:50.980
When the fire is small,
it's easy to tackle.

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:53.560
And that's where
we want to offer--

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:55.540
drones can go out and
put out the fire when

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:58.360
it's small and containable.

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:02.980
We also want to integrate
various sensors and equipments,

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:05.800
which has been installed
by various climatologists

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:08.680
and scientific organizations.

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:12.160
Social media, sentiment
analysis, and another piece

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:13.480
of our offering--

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:17.650
people who are in
the disaster areas,

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.770
they get the
information firsthand.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:20.920
They tweet about it.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.810
They post videos, images.

00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:26.740
And using that social
media integration,

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.530
we want to analyze,
and bring awareness,

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:32.540
and training of those disasters.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:36.280
These are some of the
man-made and natural disasters

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:39.490
which we cater to our platform.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:42.670
We also want to
integrate to IP 911

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:45.220
to bring active
shooter and terrorism

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:49.690
activities in real-time.

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:51.925
This is a brief video
which demonstrates

00:17:51.925 --> 00:17:54.100
the capability of our platform.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:58.990
Back in 2018, we detected
Camp and Woolsey fires

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:00.970
within minutes of it happening.

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:04.630
As you can see in this
video, we have annotations--

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:08.950
these yellow boxes being
potential fire hotspot,

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:11.830
green ones could be
false or non-event,

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:14.410
and the red are active hotspot.

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:16.840
We can also see
smoke, and cloud,

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.090
and other geological
phenomenas which

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:25.336
helps fire first responders
understand the situation.

00:18:25.336 --> 00:18:28.250
AI being a big part
of our offering,

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:31.630
this is a demonstration
of AutoML Vision.

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.820
This is the first part
of visual detection.

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:37.800
We use satellite
imagery and we're

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:40.270
handling this model using--

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:43.870
this is a short example,
being smoke cloud and clear.

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:47.930
Using AutoML Vision,
we train the model.

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:51.040
And after training, it's
available as an end point.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:54.130
Using this end point,
we integrated real-time

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:56.590
with our live feeds of images.

00:18:56.590 --> 00:19:00.700
And this is a short example of
how we can detect smoke cloud

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:02.279
with very high accuracy.

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:11.810
Another part of our visual
offering is AutoML Video.

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:14.240
We work with Google
in a beta program

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:17.630
where we use our drone
footage and train

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:23.390
it to detect smoke, fire, and
other objects in the scene.

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:28.220
Imagine a drone going to place
and give us a real-time video.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:30.290
And we pass it through
the streaming API

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:32.635
and generate actionable event.

00:19:32.635 --> 00:19:37.570
Using this-- this is the
third part of our AI strategy.

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:39.410
With this, I'll hand
it over to Jacob

00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:43.707
to talk about our roadmap.

00:19:43.707 --> 00:19:45.290
JACOB STABLER: So
in May, we are going

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:50.840
to launch in eight cities in
Orange County, California.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:55.580
From there, we are going to
create disaster awareness

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:58.820
campaigns for educational
institutes and high occupancy

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:00.200
buildings.

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:03.680
We will also be launching
later in the year in all

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:08.568
of US and Canada with
the IP 911 integration.

00:20:08.568 --> 00:20:10.610
I just wanted to say thanks
to Jason and the rest

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:11.978
of the Google team.

00:20:11.978 --> 00:20:13.520
NITIN KUMAR: And I
also want to thank

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:15.980
Google technical team,
who I have worked closely

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.090
during the beta program.

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:18.340
They have been really helpful.

00:20:18.340 --> 00:20:19.218
And thank you.

00:20:19.218 --> 00:20:20.260
JACOB STABLER: Thank you.

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:20.760
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:23.310
